Product Requirements Document: Jewelry & Gold POS System
1. Introduction
This document outlines the product requirements for a web-based Point of Sale (POS) and Inventory Management system tailored specifically for jewelry stores, with a focus on gold and precious metals. The system, inspired by the "Nagagold Store Solution," is designed to manage complex inventory, specialized transactions like buybacks and pawning, dynamic pricing, and comprehensive reporting.

2. User Personas & Roles
The system must support a role-based access control (RBAC) model to ensure security and operational integrity.

Store Owner (Owner): Has unrestricted access to all system menus, settings, and reports. This role is the only one that can configure core system settings and has ultimate authorization power.

Store Manager / Supervisor (Kepala Toko): Has access to most operational modules, including sales, purchases, inventory management, and reporting. Can perform authorizations for transactions requiring approval, such as discounts, cancellations, and item write-offs.

Sales Staff (Sales / Cashier): Has limited access, primarily to the transaction modules (Penjualan, Pembelian, Service, Pesanan). Cannot perform sensitive actions without authorization from a Manager or Owner.

3. Core Features & Functionality
3.1. Dashboard
A centralized home screen that provides a real-time overview of the store's daily activities.


Key Metrics Display: Show daily totals for Sales, Orders (Pesanan), and Services. 



Transaction Graph: Display a monthly chart comparing sales and purchase volumes. 


Quick Access Menu: Provide buttons for critical end-of-day functions: Tutup Toko (Close Store/End of Day), Ganti Password (Change Password), and Log Out. 


System Date Display: Clearly show the current system date, which advances only after the Tutup Toko process is completed. 

3.2. Data Master (Core Data Configuration)
This module is the foundation of the system, where all fundamental data entities are defined before transactions can occur.

Master Group: Defines main item categories (e.g., Gold, Platinum, Diamonds).


Fields: Group Code, Group Name, Group Type (e.g., TUA/MUDA for different gold purity classes), Price (the base price for 100% purity), Modal Price (cost price). 



Master Jenis: Defines item types within a Master Group (e.g., Ring, Necklace, Bracelet). 


Fields: Linked Group Code, Type Code, Type Name. 

Master Gudang (Warehouse): Defines physical or logical locations for inventory.


Logic: Must include a default TOKO (Store) warehouse from which sales are made. Other examples include 

BELI (Buyback), CUCI (Cleaning), REPARASI (Repair). 

Master Baki (Tray): Defines display or storage trays where items are kept.


Fields: Linked Warehouse Code, Tray Code, Tray Name, Tray Weight (empty), Tag Weight (weight of a single barcode tag). 



Logic: This is critical for the "Weigh Tray" stock-taking feature. A special 

BAKISEM (Temporary Tray) must exist for staging newly added items before printing barcodes. 



Master Bandrol (Tag): A global setting to define the weight of a single barcode/price tag, which is used as a deduction during mass weighing. 



Master Sales: Manages employee data for tracking sales performance and transaction ownership. 


Master Marketplace: Defines online sales channels (e.g., Tokopedia, Shopee) for tracking off-site sales. 

Master Kondisi Pembelian (Buyback Condition): Defines rules for price deductions when buying items back from customers.


Fields: Condition Name (e.g., MULUS/Good, RUSAK/Damaged), Price Deduction (fixed amount), or Percentage Deduction. 


Master Bank & Rekening: Manages the store's bank and account details for non-cash transactions. 


Parameter Settings:

Parameter Harga Emas (Gold Price): A dedicated interface to update the base price and cost price for each Master Group. This change must automatically propagate and update the price of all inventory items within that group. 



Parameter Ongkos (Service/Crafting Fees): A system to define and apply additional costs to items based on internal codes, which can be a fixed nominal value or a percentage. 


Other Parameters: Manage transaction codes, item condition codes, loyalty point rules, and custom receipt notes. 




3.3. Barang (Inventory Management)
The core module for managing the store's physical and digital inventory.

Tambah Barang (Add Item):

Must allow adding items with extensive details: Photo (from file or webcam), Internal Code, Item Name, Exact Weight (

Berat Asli), Billing Weight (Berat), Purity (Kadar Cetak), and details for any attached attributes like gemstones (Attribute Name, Weight, Price). 




A "copy" function must be available to quickly duplicate an item's details for adding multiple similar items. 

Hancur Barang (Write-off Item):

A function to remove an item from inventory for reasons like damage or input error. 

This action must require 

authorization from a Manager/Owner and generate a printable PDF as proof. 



Pindah Barang (Move Item):

Allows moving one or more items between trays (

Baki) or warehouses (Gudang). 

Includes a "Move Whole Tray" function to transfer all contents of one tray to another. 

Timbang Baki (Weigh Tray):

A critical stock-taking feature. The user selects a tray, and the system displays the expected total weight based on its contents. 

The user enters the actual physical weight from a scale.

The system calculates and displays any discrepancy (

Selisih). 

Formula: Expected Net Gold Weight = SUM(Item Weights)

Formula: Expected Gross Weight = Expected Net Gold Weight + Tray Weight + (Item Quantity * Tag Weight)


Stock Opname: A formal process to adjust system stock to match physical reality, often used when discrepancies are found during Timbang Baki. 

3.4. Transaksi (Transactions)
This section covers all customer-facing financial activities. A key requirement is that all transactions are created in a pending state and only become final after being processed in the Validasi module.

Penjualan (Sales):

Initiate a sale by selecting a customer (or creating a new one) and the salesperson. 

Add items to the cart by scanning their barcodes. 

Support for price or weight adjustments, which must trigger an 

authorization request if the new price is below the system price. 


Multi-Payment: Must accept various payment methods for a single transaction (Cash, Debit, Transfer, Credit Card). 

Tukar Tambah (Trade-in): A feature to accept a customer's old jewelry as partial payment. This opens a sub-form to process a 

Pembelian (Buyback) within the sales transaction. 

Titipan (Layaway / Down Payment):

A specific sales flow where a customer pays a deposit (

DP) for an item. 

The system must automatically move the item to a 

TITIP warehouse to make it unavailable for other sales. 

A function to retrieve the 

Titipan transaction later for final payment and pickup (Ambil Titipan). 

Pembelian (Buyback):

Process for buying jewelry back from customers. 

If the item has a previous sales receipt from the system, scanning it should auto-fill the details. 

The user must select the item's condition (

Kondisi Pembelian), which automatically applies the correct price/percentage deduction. 

The bought-back item must be placed in the 

BELI warehouse. 



Pesanan (Custom Orders):

Handles orders for items not currently in stock. 

Capture detailed order specifications: Photo, category, desired weight, crafting fees (

Ongkos), and description. 


Order Fulfillment (Ambil Pesanan): This is a critical two-step workflow:

The finished custom item must first be formally added to the system's inventory using the 

Tambah Data Barang menu, with a specific "Barang Pesanan" function that links it to the original order number. 


The user then retrieves the order in the main sales screen via an "Ambil Pesanan" button, which pulls the transaction details and allows the customer to make the final payment against the newly created item. 

Service (Repairs):

Manages non-sale service requests like cleaning or repairs. 

Capture customer details, item description, photo, and service fees. 

Track the status of the service from received to finished to picked-up. 

Hutang (Pawning):

Manages transactions where customers pawn items for a loan. 

Capture item details, loan amount, monthly interest rate (

Bunga/Bulan), and loan duration to automatically calculate the due date. 


Provide functions for 

Pelunasan Hutang (repayment) and Peremajaan (loan renewal/extension). 


3.5. Validasi (Transaction Validation)
A mandatory control gate for all major transactions.


Unified Interface: A central screen where a Manager or Owner can view all unvalidated Sales, Purchases, and Pawning transactions. 


Approval Workflow: The user must select and approve each transaction. 



System Logic: Only after validation are the following actions triggered:

Inventory stock levels are updated (decreased for sales, increased for purchases). 



Financial records and reports are updated. 



The official receipt/nota is printed. 

3.6. Laporan (Reporting)
The system must provide a comprehensive and filterable reporting suite with export capabilities to PDF and Excel.


Inventory Reports: Detail, Summary, Added Items, Moved Items, Written-off Items, Weighing Discrepancies, Stock Opname Results. 






Sales Reports: Sales by Margin, Items Sold, Sales by Salesperson, Trade-in Items, Canceled Sales, Marketplace Sales, Layaway/DP Reports. 






Purchase (Buyback) Reports: All buyback transactions, Canceled Buybacks, Buybacks by Salesperson. 




Pawning Reports: Active Loans, Repaid Loans, Canceled Loans, Loan Stock. 





Financial Reports: Cash Flow (Cash), Non-Cash Flow (Bank), End Of Day (EOD) Summary. 




Audit & Control Reports: Authorization Log, Gold Price Change History. 


4. Non-Functional Requirements

Security: Case-sensitive login credentials.  A robust authorization framework for sensitive operations is mandatory.

Data Integrity: All major state changes (stock, finance) are tied to the Validasi process to prevent errors. All significant actions must generate a printable proof document (e.g., 

Bukti Pindah Barang, Bukti Hancur Barang). 


Usability: The system must be a web-based application accessible through a standard browser. It must support and integrate with hardware for efficient operation.

Hardware Integration:

Barcode Scanner: For all item-related transactions (sales, inventory checks, etc.).

Webcam: For capturing images of items for inventory, orders, and services. 

Receipt/Label Printer: For printing sales receipts, pawn agreements, and barcode labels.

Performance: The system should handle real-time price updates across the entire inventory without significant lag. Report generation should be optimized.
